Certainly! If you want to bypass the user ID constraint, you can use a boolean condition that's always true. For example:

SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE user_id = 42 OR 1=1;

This OR 1=1 part ensures the WHERE clause always evaluates to true, so you'll retrieve all rows.

Absolutely. You can append a comment marker to ignore the remaining part of the query. For MySQL, use `-- ` (double dash and a space):

SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE user_id = 42 OR 1=1 -- ;

Anything after the double dash will be treated as a comment and skipped by the database.

You can utilize a UNION-based injection. For instance:

SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = 1 UNION SELECT password FROM admin -- ;

This merges results from both queries, letting you pull columns from the 'admin' table as well.

Try using a Boolean-based blind injection. For example:

SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = 10 AND 1=1 -- ;

If you change 1=1 to 1=0 and the response changes (e.g., no results), you'll know your input is being used in the query.

For error-based injection, you can use functions that produce errors, like this:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1 AND (SELECT 1/0) -- ;

The division by zero will trigger a database error, which sometimes reveals useful information in the error message.

You can employ time-based techniques. For example, in MySQL:

SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE name = 'admin' AND SLEEP(5) -- ;

If the page takes noticeably longer to load, your injected SLEEP() function is being executed.

Good idea! You can obfuscate injection statements by mixing case or using URL encoding. For example:

SeLeCt * FrOm users WhErE id=1 Or 1=1 -- ;

Or encode spaces as %20 and other characters as needed to mask the payload.
